 What it means: Write HTML-like code inside JavaScript.
 Example: <div><h1>Welcome</h1><p>JSX is easy!</p></div>
 Why we use: Makes writing UI readable and maintainable, lets you embed
JavaScript logic in HTML easily.
Program:
function App() {
return (
<div>
<h1>Welcome to JSX</h1>
<p>JSX makes writing UI easier!</p>
</div>
);
}
export default App; 5(c) Components (Function & Class)
 What it means: Build reusable building blocks of UI.
1. Function Component – Simple, returns JSX, no state (basic).
2. Class Component – Can have state, lifecycle methods, more powerful.
 Nesting Components: Using one component inside another to create a hierarchy of 
UI.
 Why we use: Helps organize code, avoid repetition, and make apps scalable.
Visualizing Components
App (Main Component)
│
└─ Welcome (Class Component)
│
└─ Greeting (Function Component)
 Each box is a component.
 Arrows show nesting, how small components combine to form complex UI.
Program:
import React from "react";
// Function Component
function Greeting() {
 return <h2>Hello from Function Component</h2>;
}
// Class Component
class Welcome extends React.Component {
 render() {
 return (
 <div>
 <h2>Hello from Class Component</h2>
 <Greeting />
 </div>
 );
 }
} // Main Component
function App() {
 return (
 <div>
 <h1>React Components Example</h1>
 <Welcome />
 </div>
 );
}
export default App;
